-- title:
--   Narrow ray class character of a prime-degree cyclic extension
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ and $E$ be number fields with $E$ an $F$-algebra which is Galois over $F$, and let $\ell$ be a prime number with $[E:F] = \ell$. The assertion is that there exists a nonzero ideal $\mathfrak f$ of $\mathcal O_F$ which is an admissible modulus for $E/F$, i.e. $\mathfrak f \neq 0$ and for every finite prime $v$ of $F$ (a point of the height-one spectrum of $\mathcal O_F$) for which the inertia subgroup in $\mathrm{Gal}(E/F)$ of the chosen prime `primeAbove F E v` of $\mathcal O_E$ above $v$ is nontrivial, $v^{\,4 e(v\mid 2) + 2 e(v\mid 3) + 1}$ divides $\mathfrak f$, the exponents being the ramification indices at $v$ of the ideals generated by $2$ and by $3$; and that there exists a group homomorphism $\eta$ from the narrow ray class group of $F$ modulo $\mathfrak f$ — the quotient of the group of invertible fractional ideals having valuation $0$ at every prime dividing $\mathfrak f$ by the subgroup cut out on it by `narrowRaySubgroup` — to $\mathbb C^\times$, such that for every prime $w$ of $\mathcal O_E$ whose underlying prime $v = w \cap \mathcal O_F$ does not divide $\mathfrak f$, the multiplicative order of $\eta$ evaluated at the class of $v$ equals the residue degree `Ideal.inertiaDeg'` of $w$ over $v$.
--
--   This is Artin reciprocity for a cyclic extension of prime degree, packaged so that the resulting narrow ray class character detects the splitting type of each prime away from the modulus by its exact order: the character is trivial at completely split primes and of order $\ell$ at inert primes. It serves the cyclic base change step of the Langlands–Tunnell argument, being used in the constructions of twists for base-changed forms in degrees two and three.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_LanglandsTunnell_P2_Artin_exists_rayClassChar_orderOf_eq_inertiaDeg_of_finrank_eq_prime.lean

